### A · Governance and representation

1. **Governance** is the manner in which power is exercised in managing a country's economic and social resources (**World Bank**, ==1992==).
2. The World Bank's ==1989== report on Sub-Saharan Africa spoke of a "**crisis of governance**".
3. **UNESCAP** lists **eight** characteristics of good governance.
4. The eight are **participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus orientation, equity and inclusiveness, effectiveness and efficiency, and accountability**.
5. **Article 21** of the **Universal Declaration of Human Rights** (==1948==) gives everyone the right to take part in government.
6. **Article 25** of the **ICCPR** (==1966==) protects the right to vote and be elected.
7. **Article 7** of **CEDAW** (==1979==) requires equality for women in political and public life.
8. **Article 4(1)** of CEDAW allows **temporary special measures** such as quotas.
9. **Hanna Pitkin** wrote *The Concept of Representation* in ==1967==.
10. Pitkin's four types are **formal, descriptive, substantive and symbolic** representation.
11. **Descriptive** representation means representatives resemble those they represent.
12. **Substantive** representation means representatives act for the interests of the represented.
13. **Anne Phillips** wrote *The Politics of Presence* in ==1995==.
14. **Rosabeth Moss Kanter** described women under about 15% of a group as **tokens** (==1977==).
15. **Drude Dahlerup** applied **critical mass** to politics in ==1988==.
16. Critical mass is usually put at about **30%**.
17. ECOSOC endorsed a **30%** target for women in leadership in ==1990==.
18. Beijing Platform Critical Area **G** is "**women in power and decision-making**" (==1995==).
19. **Childs and Krook** (==2009==) stressed **critical actors** over critical mass.
20. SDG target **5.5** calls for women's full participation and equal opportunities for leadership.

### B · The suffrage movement

21. **Suffrage** means the **right to vote** (MCQ 2019).
22. **New Zealand** was the first country to give women the vote, in ==1893== (MCQ 2024).
23. New Zealand's suffrage petitions were led by **Kate Sheppard**.
24. **Finland** (==1906==) was the first European country to give women the vote and the first to let them stand for parliament.
25. **Australia** gave women the federal vote in ==1902==, but Aboriginal women only in 1962.
26. The **Seneca Falls Convention** met on 19–20 July ==1848==.
27. Seneca Falls adopted the **Declaration of Sentiments**, drafted by **Elizabeth Cady Stanton**.
28. **Lucretia Mott** co-organised Seneca Falls.
29. **Frederick Douglass** supported the vote resolution at Seneca Falls.
30. The **National Woman Suffrage Association** of Stanton and **Susan B. Anthony** was founded in ==1869==.
31. **Lucy Stone** led the **American Woman Suffrage Association** (==1869==).
32. The two American associations merged as **NAWSA** in ==1890==.
33. **Wyoming** Territory gave women the vote in ==1869==.
34. The **19th Amendment** gave American women the vote in ==1920==.
35. **Alice Paul** led the militant National Woman's Party.
36. **John Stuart Mill** presented a women's suffrage petition to Parliament in ==1866==.
37. Mill wrote *The Subjection of Women* in ==1869==.
38. **Millicent Fawcett** led the **NUWSS** (==1897==).
39. **Emmeline Pankhurst** founded the **WSPU** in ==1903==.
40. The WSPU's motto was "**Deeds, not words**".
41. The *Daily Mail* coined the word "**suffragette**" in ==1906==.
42. The "**Cat and Mouse Act**" of ==1913== released and re-arrested hunger-striking suffragettes.
43. **Emily Wilding Davison** died after the Epsom Derby in ==1913==.
44. Britain's **Representation of the People Act** (==1918==) gave the vote to women over **30** with property.
45. The **Equal Franchise Act** (==1928==) gave British women the vote at **21**.
46. **France** gave women the vote in ==1944==; **Switzerland** at federal level in ==1971==.
47. **Saudi Arabia** let women vote in municipal elections in ==2015==.
48. **Sri Lanka** (Ceylon) introduced universal adult franchise in ==1931==.
49. **Madras** (==1921==) was the first Indian province to enfranchise women.
50. The **Government of India Act 1935** reserved seats for women in the legislatures.

### C · Women as voters

51. The minimum voting age in Pakistan is **18** (MCQ 2016), lowered from 21 in ==2002==.
52. A voter in Pakistan must hold a **CNIC** issued by **NADRA**.
53. Pakistan had **128.58 million** registered voters in ==2024==.
54. Women were **59.32 million** (46.1%) of registered voters in 2024.
55. The voter **registration gender gap** was ==9.94 million== in 2024.
56. The registration gap fell from ==12.8%== (2013) to ==7.7%== (2024).
57. Women's turnout in 2024 was about ==43%==, against about ==52%== for men.
58. Women cast **24.4 million** of 58.9 million votes in 2024.
59. The turnout gender gap was widest in **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa** in 2024.
60. **Section 9** of the **Elections Act 2017** lets the ECP void a poll where women's turnout is under **10%**.
61. In ==2015== the ECP annulled a by-election in **Lower Dir** where women were kept from voting.
62. **FAFEN** is the **Free and Fair Election Network**, which monitors elections in Pakistan.
63. **Fatima Jinnah** contested the ==1965== presidential election against **Ayub Khan**.
64. Pakistan's first direct nationwide general election on adult franchise was held in ==1970==.

### D · Women as candidates

65. A candidate for a National Assembly seat must be at least **25** years old.
66. A **graduation** condition for candidates applied from ==2002== to ==2008==.
67. **Section 206** of the Elections Act 2017 requires parties to give **5%** of general-seat tickets to women.
68. **306** women contested general National Assembly seats in ==2024==.
69. **11** women won general National Assembly seats in 2024.
70. **8** women won general National Assembly seats in ==2018==.
71. "**Electables**" are candidates with land, clan and money networks favoured by parties.
72. **Benazir Bhutto** became Pakistan's first woman prime minister in ==1988== (MCQ 2018).
73. Benazir Bhutto was assassinated in Rawalpindi on ==27 December 2007==.
74. **Maryam Nawaz** became Pakistan's first woman chief minister (Punjab) in ==2024==.

### E · Women as representatives

75. The National Assembly has **336** seats: **266** general, **60** women and **10** non-Muslim.
76. The Senate has **96** seats, **17** of them reserved for women.
77. The Punjab Assembly has **66** reserved seats for women.
78. Reserved seats are filled by **proportional representation** from party priority lists.
79. The first Constituent Assembly had **two** women (MCQs 2016, 2024).
80. The two were **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Shaista Ikramullah**.
81. **Fahmida Mirza** became the first woman Speaker of the National Assembly in ==2008== (MCQs 2016, 2019).
82. The **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** was formed in ==2008==.
83. **Hina Rabbani Khar** became Pakistan's first woman foreign minister in ==2011==.
84. **Tehmina Janjua** became Pakistan's first woman foreign secretary in ==2017== (MCQ 2018).
85. **Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** became the first woman governor (Sindh) in ==1973==.
86. **Justice Ayesha Malik** became the first woman Supreme Court judge in ==2022==.
87. In ==2025== Pakistan had **no** women federal ministers (WEF).

### F · Quotas and their history

88. A **gender quota** requires a minimum share or number of women in a body.
89. The three main quota types are **reserved seats, legislated candidate quotas and voluntary party quotas**.
90. A "**zipper**" list alternates women and men candidates.
91. **Dahlerup** contrasted the "**incremental track**" with the "**fast track**" to equality.
92. More than **130** countries use political gender quotas.
93. **Rwanda** has the world's highest share of women in parliament — over **60%**.
94. **Norway** adopted a **40%** quota for women on company boards in ==2003==.
95. The **1956** Constitution reserved **10** seats for women (5 East, 5 West).
96. The **1962** Constitution reserved **6** seats for women.
97. The **1973** Constitution reserved **10** seats for women for ten years or three elections.
98. Women's reserved seats were raised to **20** in ==1985==.
99. Reserved seats **lapsed** after the ==1988== election.
100. The elections of **1990, 1993 and 1997** had no reserved seats for women.
101. The **Devolution of Power Plan** (==2000==) reserved **33%** of local seats for women (MCQ 2016).
102. About **36,000** women councillors were elected in ==2000–01==.
103. The **Legal Framework Order 2002** created **60** reserved seats in an enlarged **342**-member National Assembly.
104. Sixty of 342 seats is about **17%** — the "17 percent quota" (MCQ 2016; 2021 question).
105. The **17th Amendment** (==2003==) validated the LFO.
106. The LFO reserved **128** provincial seats and **17** Senate seats for women.
107. After the **25th Amendment** (==2018==) the National Assembly fell to **336** seats, keeping **60** for women.
108. Pakistan reserves **10%** of federal government posts for women.

### G · Impact and leadership

109. **Chattopadhyay and Duflo** (==2004==) found that women panchayat leaders invested more in **drinking water**.
110. India's **73rd and 74th Amendments** (==1992==) reserved one-third of local seats for women.
111. The **glass ceiling** is an invisible barrier stopping women reaching the top.
112. The **glass cliff** — women appointed to lead in crises — was named by **Ryan and Haslam** (==2005==) (MCQ 2026).
113. **Eagly and Carli** (==2007==) described women's path to leadership as a **labyrinth**.
114. The "**queen bee**" term was coined by Staines, Tavris and Jayaratne in ==1974==.
115. "**Women hold up half the sky**" is a saying associated with **Mao Zedong** (2018 question).
116. **UNSCR 1325** (==2000==) calls for women's participation in peace and security.

### H · South Asia

117. **Sirimavo Bandaranaike** of Sri Lanka became the world's first woman prime minister in ==1960==.
118. **Nepal**'s **2015** constitution requires one-third women in the federal parliament.
119. **Bangladesh** has had **50** reserved seats for women since ==2011==.
120. India's **Constitution (106th Amendment) Act 2023** reserves **one-third** of Lok Sabha seats for women after the next delimitation.
